# Hoserva storage-target boot ordering (doc 02 §1, Q69, issue #372):
# reaches disk.StorageGate the moment a block device carrying a
# filesystem appears — a reseated cable, or the disk a degraded boot's
# own missing-and-unacknowledged state left waiting for — instead of only
# at the next reboot or an unrelated share edit. `systemctl --no-block
# reload` sends hoserva.service SIGHUP (ExecReload=,
# packaging/debian/hoserva.service); cmd/hoservad's own SIGHUP handler
# (installReloadHandler, cmd/hoservad/reload.go) re-lists disks and
# re-evaluates the gate from that list alone — it never reads a data
# disk's own content, so this stays event-driven, never a timer walking a
# data disk (CLAUDE.md).
#
# debian/rules installs this file itself, as
# /lib/udev/rules.d/90-hoserva-storage.rules — never through
# dh_installudev's own debian/hoserva.udev naming convention, and the
# "90-" prefix is deliberate, not cosmetic: udev evaluates same-priority
# rule *files* in filename order, and Debian's own udev package ships
# /lib/udev/rules.d/60-persistent-storage.rules, whose own
# `IMPORT{builtin}="blkid"` is what actually sets ENV{ID_FS_UUID} in the
# first place (confirmed directly with `udevadm test`). This file's own
# name has to sort after that one, or the condition below always sees an
# empty ENV{ID_FS_UUID} and never matches.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="block", ENV{ID_FS_UUID}!="", RUN+="/usr/bin/systemctl --no-block reload hoserva.service"
